message.reasoning_content:
Build succeeded. Now inspect generated HTML. Need jsdom. Let me install jsdom in the probe dir via npm (registry.npmjs.org works).

Let me install and run a script.

Actually, maybe there's a lighter way: the compiled JS is a UMD-ish `(function(scope){...})(this)`. Elm compiled output is a script that, given `document`, mounts to #app. Let me use jsdom.

npm i jsdom — might take a while. Let me try.

Alternative: use `happy-dom`? Also npm.

Let me install jsdom with a longer timeout.

Actually, we could avoid jsdom by using elm's `Browser.element` node setup... no.

Let me just install jsdom.

Also possible: use `linkedom` (lighter). But jsdom is standard.

Let me do npm init -y && npm i jsdom.

Note: /tmp/elmui-probe — npm install there.

Let me write the node script too.

The elm.js: elm compiles `Browser.element` program that on load expects `<div id="app">`... Actually Browser.element doesn't auto-mount; it exports `Elm.Main.init({node})`. So script must call `Elm.Main.init({ node: document.getElementById('app') })`. The compiled UMD sets `this.Elm`. In jsdom runScripts, `window.Elm`.

Let me write:
```js
const { JSDOM } = require('jsdom');
const fs = require('fs');
const code = fs.readFileSync('elm.js','utf8');
const dom = new JSDOM('<!DOCTYPE html><html><body><div id="app"></div></body></html>', { runScripts: 'dangerously' });
dom.window.eval(code);
dom.window.Elm.Main.init({ node: dom.window.document.getElementById('app') });
setTimeout(()=>{
  console.log(dom.window.document.getElementById('app').innerHTML);
}, 100);
```
Elm init may need `requestAnimationFrame`. jsdom has it? Newer jsdom has rAF. Elm runtime uses `requestAnimationFrame` for scheduling; jsdom provides it in recent versions. If not, polyfill.

Let me try.

Let me run npm install first.

